### Revised Rule

The input grid contains three distinct objects: left object (color 6), middle column object (color 4), and right object (color 8). The middle column object is a vertical line of color 4 spanning all rows (always present in column 3). The left and right objects are contiguous regions of colors 6 and 8 respectively, possibly disconnected across rows.

The output is a 3×3 grid where:
- Rows correspond to the three rows of the input.
- Columns correspond to the three objects: left (6), middle (4), right (8).

For each cell (r, c) in the output:
- If c is the middle column (object color 4): output 2 if **both** the left object (6) and the right object (8) have at least one cell in row r of the input. Otherwise output 0.
- If c is the left column (object color 6): output 2 if the left object (6) has at least one cell in row r **and** also has at least one cell in the next row (r+1, cyclically, so row 2’s next row is row 0) **within the same connected component** (i.e., the cells in rows r and r+1 are connected via adjacency in the input grid). Otherwise output 0.
- If c is the right column (object color 8): output 2 if the right object (8) has at least one cell in row r **and** also has at least one cell in the next row (r+1, cyclically) **within the same connected component**. Otherwise output 0.

This rule uses object-level connectivity and row presence, and explains all training examples.

